---

<Base
  lang="en"
  page="overview"
  eyebrow="A home system, built for a hackathon"
  title="An activity on paper, invented for one person"
  description="Lanternina invents an activity for an adolescent, has a parent approve it, and runs it on paper and e-paper. Language, vision and image inference runs in Microsoft Foundry."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Lanternina invents an activity for an adolescent, has a parent approve it, and then runs it in
    a real room — on paper, on e-paper displays, and on physical buttons. It reads what comes back
    in handwriting, decides what happens next from that, and stops at an hour the parent set.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mini-PC in a house drives two e-paper displays, an inkjet and a flatbed scanner. Every
    language and vision call goes to Microsoft Foundry in the EU, so inference requires cloud
    connectivity. The house holds the clock and sealing keys, initiates each request and controls
    when work begins.
  </p>

  <figure class="band">
    <img
      src="/images/hero.jpg"
      width="1800"
      height="771"
      alt="A 7.5-inch e-paper display in a 3D printed frame, showing an engraving."
    />
    <figcaption>
      One of two displays. The frame is 3D printed and the unit was assembled by hand; the picture
      on it was generated an hour earlier from a theme the parent wrote.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>How an activity goes</h2>

  <p>
    The sequence begins in the parent panel. The mini-PC then runs the approved activity on its own
    clock.
  </p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <b>A parent reviews the ideas and approves one.</b> The panel shows the overview, themes and
      script for each idea. The parent can edit the brief before approval and sets the hour by which
      the activity must end.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The API turns the approved brief into an activity.</b> The model router sends the brief to
      Microsoft Foundry. One response contains the activity graph, three lengths for each moment,
      four levels of help and a route to the ending from every point.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The API validates and stores the activity.</b> Structural checks verify its routes, objects
      and answers. A failed check gives the model one repair attempt. Azure AI Content Safety then
      screens the text, and the completed activity is stored for that household.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The mini-PC collects the activity.</b> It polls the device API on its own schedule. The API
      returns the waiting activity in that HTTPS response, and the mini-PC stores it locally before
      preparing the first page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The mini-PC prints the first page.</b> The router sends the screened text and a page
      description to an image model. The model draws the complete A4 sheet, and the print service
      sends it to the inkjet over IPP.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The scanner returns the completed page.</b> A vision model receives the scan and the
      original image. It compares them and returns a reading of the marks that were added.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The reading selects the next moment.</b> The execution service follows the corresponding
      edge in the activity graph and prepares the next display or page. Thirty minutes before the
      hour set by the parent, it enters the planned route from the current moment to the ending.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What comes out, and what goes back</h2>

  <p>
    The page below was drawn whole by an image model, from words that had already been screened.
    Somebody writes on it and puts it back on the scanner glass; the reading decides the next
    moments, which are written while the sheet is still on the table.
  </p>

  <div class="pair">
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/page.jpg"
        width="919"
        height="1300"
        loading="lazy"
        alt="A printed page, blank."
      />
      <figcaption>Handed over. Unedited model output, 19–33 s to draw.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/page-written.jpg"
        width="866"
        height="1300"
        loading="lazy"
        alt="The same page, filled in by hand."
      />
      <figcaption>
        Came back. A simulated hand filled this example to exercise the complete run automatically.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    The vision result has two values: <b>marks</b> and <b>blank</b>. This vocabulary prevents the
    reading path from turning somebody's marks into a count or score. After the next moment is
    selected, the reading type closes the pickle, copy and cache paths out of memory.
  </p>

